.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt MEI 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mei
.Nd Intel Management Engine Interface driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following line in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device mei"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mei_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Intel Management Engine
.Pq ME
is an embedded controller in Intel chipsets that runs its own firmware
independently of the host CPU.
The Host Embedded Controller Interface
.Pq HECI
PCI function is a message channel between the host and the firmware
services running on the ME, known as ME clients.
Each ME client is identified by a protocol GUID.
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver resets the channel, negotiates the version of the host bus
message protocol with the firmware and enumerates the ME clients.
It then provides a character device through which user programs can
connect to an ME client and exchange messages with it.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports the Intel Converged Security and Management Engine
.Pq CSME
HECI function 1 on Intel 100 Series
.Pq Sunrise Point
PCH and later chipsets, up to Lunar Lake and the Intel 800 Series PCH,
including Alder Lake.
.Sh DEVICE INTERFACE
Each open of
.Pa /dev/mei0
creates one host client.
Before any data can be transferred, the host client must be connected to
an ME client with the
.Dv IOCTL_MEI_CONNECT_CLIENT
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
which takes a pointer to the following structure, defined in
.In dev/mei/meiio.h :
.Bd -literal
struct mei_client {
	uint32_t	max_msg_length;
	uint8_t		protocol_version;
	uint8_t		reserved[3];
};

struct mei_connect_client_data {
	union {
		/* in: protocol GUID of the ME client, GUID byte order */
		uint8_t			in_client_uuid[16];
		/* out: properties of the connected ME client */
		struct mei_client	out_client_properties;
	};
};
.Ed
.Pp
On input,
.Va in_client_uuid
holds the protocol GUID of the ME client in GUID byte order: the first
field is stored as 4 bytes in little endian order, the second and third
fields as 2 bytes each in little endian order, and the remaining 8 bytes
in the order they are written.
On successful return,
.Va out_client_properties
holds the maximum message length accepted by the ME client and the
version of the protocol it implements.
.Pp
Once connected, each
.Xr write 2
sends one message of at most
.Va max_msg_length
bytes to the ME client, and each
.Xr read 2
returns one message received from it.
If the buffer passed to
.Xr read 2
is shorter than the message, the remainder of the message is returned
by the next
.Xr read 2 .
.Xr poll 2
is supported.
A connection lasts until the file descriptor is closed.
.Pp
The ioctl number, the structure layout and the read and write semantics
match those of the Linux mei character device, so existing tools written
for that interface can be used.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following read-only variables are available:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.mei.%d.fwsts1 No .. Va dev.mei.%d.fwsts6
The six firmware status registers of the ME.
.It Va dev.mei.%d.hbm_version
The version of the host bus message protocol negotiated with the
firmware.
.It Va dev.mei.%d.clients
The list of ME clients reported by the firmware.
For each client it shows the client address, the protocol GUID,
the maximum message length, whether the client uses a single receive
buffer and whether it is a fixed address client.
.It Va dev.mei.%d.state
The state of the host interface.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/mei0" -compact
.It Pa /dev/mei0
character device of the first
.Nm
instance
.El
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Dv IOCTL_MEI_CONNECT_CLIENT
request fails if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
No ME client with the given GUID exists, or the ME client does not
accept connections.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The ME client is already connected, or it has no free connections.
.It Bq Er EALREADY
The file descriptor is already connected.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The firmware did not answer the connect request in time.
.El
.Pp
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
The file descriptor is not connected to an ME client.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
The connection was lost, for example because the ME was reset.
The device must be closed and opened again.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
The message passed to
.Xr write 2
is longer than
.Va max_msg_length .
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
The file descriptor is in non-blocking mode
.Pq Dv O_NONBLOCK
and no message is available, or the message cannot be sent yet.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pci 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Mx 4.2 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
driver was written by
.An Lucas Holt .
.Sh CAVEATS
The driver keeps the device in the D0 power state and does not use D0i3
power gating.
It does not support DMA rings, extended message headers or dynamic
addition of ME clients.
